/**

\page design How it works

\section design_layers Layers

  - FLTK's Fl_Dockable_Group moves a group out of its window into a window
    FLTK makes for it, floats it, and tells docking targets about it as it is
    dragged and dropped (the FL_DOCK_ENTER, FL_DOCK_DRAG, FL_DOCK_LEAVE,
    FL_DOCK_RELEASE and FL_UNDOCK events, and
    Fl_Dockable_Group::handle_docking_target()).  It is the only part that
    talks to the window system, so Wayland's toplevel drag works through it.
  - This library decides where a panel goes: places along the borders of a
    center, tabs, stacks, captive floating windows, stripes, maximize, and the
    layout as data.

\section design_classes Classes

  - Fl_Dockable_Area, an Fl_Tile: the center, the docked places in a list per
    side, the four stripes, and a placeholder or hint box while a panel is
    dragged over it.  It is the docking target for panels.
  - Fl_Dockable_Panel, an Fl_Dockable_Group: a title bar above a body group
    holding the application's widgets.  The title bar is the group's drag
    widget.  In tabs the title bar is hidden and the tab drags the panel.
  - Tab groups (Fl_Tabs) and stacks (Fl_Tile), made and deleted by the area
    as panels join and leave a place.  A stack along a place holds panels,
    tabs, or stacks across it; a stack across holds panels or tabs.
  - Stripes: buttons along a border.
  - Fl_Dockable_Layer: a group over the area holding only captive floating
    windows; events elsewhere in it fall through to the area.

The tab groups, stacks, stripes and title bars are the area's own and not
part of the interface.

\section design_places Places

Every panel has an Fl_Dockable_Place, which outlives the panel's being shown:
side, order along the side, size across the side, sub (order along a stack in
the place) and extent, cross (order across a part of the stack) and cross
extent, docked or floating, floating position, stripe and stripe order,
auto-hide, and shown.  Places with the same side and order share one docked
place; with the same sub too they share a part of its stack; with the same
cross too they are tabs.  Panels that share one place object take turns:
showing one hides the other.

When the user moves a panel, its place takes an order (or sub, or cross) no
other place on that side has, docked or not, so a hidden panel shown later
does not join it as a tab.

\section design_layout Layout

Fl_Dockable_Area::relayout() gives the stripes with buttons the area's edges,
then the columns at the left and right the full height inside them and the
rows at the top and bottom the center's width.  The center takes what is
left, never less than its minimum.  In a window too small for them the docked
places shrink in proportion; their places keep their sizes for when there is
room.  A maximized place takes everything; the others are hidden, and the
center is moved a pixel outside the area rather than hidden, as hiding an
OpenGL window loses its context on some systems.

Sizes the user drags borders to are written back to the places as the drag
ends, but not while a place is maximized or the docked places are shrunk.

\section design_subwindows Subwindows

A shown subwindow, such as an Fl_Gl_Window, cannot move to another window on
macOS, and a visible subwindow of a window not shown cannot be resized.  A
panel hides its subwindows before it changes window or is resized outside a
shown window, and shows them again after.

\section design_events Events

The area puts a function in front of Fl::event_dispatch(): a click anywhere
but on a panel slid out from a stripe, or its button, slides it away, and
Escape does too.  A panel's dock_shortcut() works from any of the
application's windows through an Fl::add_handler() function.

\section design_application What the application provides

Places, panels and the default layout; the captive layer, if panels should
float inside its window; its own code for opening, hiding and closing a
panel; and saving the layout when the area's callback says it changed.

*/
